Below are the columns that are (very likely) needed to train the daily‑sales regression model, to forecast the four target days (2018‑12‑05 … 2018‑12‑08) and then to compute their 5‑day symmetric moving–averages.  A short remark explains how/why each column would be used and –when useful– which filter(s) would be applied.

Relevant columns and possible filters  
• product_category_name_translation.product_category_name_english : to find the literal ‘toys’ translation.  
• product_category_name_translation.product_category_name        : Portuguese name that appears in the products table –needed for the join.  
• products.product_category_name                                  : join key that tells whether a product belongs to the “toys” family.  
• products.product_id                                             : join key that lets us pull toy products out of order_items.  
• order_items.product_id                                          : link to a toy product.  
• order_items.order_id                                            : link to orders table (to get the purchase date).  
• orders.order_id                                                 : join key.  
• orders.order_purchase_timestamp                                 : UTC‑time stamp that will be truncated to date() and used as the x‑axis in our regression.  
• (Derived) date(orders.order_purchase_timestamp)                 : sales date.  
Conditions that will be used in most queries  
– product_category_name_translation.product_category_name_english  = 'toys'  
– date(orders.order_purchase_timestamp) between '2017-01-01' and '2018-08-29'  

The ten queries below gradually explore the data, build the daily toy‑sales series, estimate the linear‑regression parameters, project the series into December 2018, and finally obtain the four requested 5‑day symmetric moving averages and their sum.  
(Every query is self‑contained, uses DISTINCT where it makes sense, and is limited to 20 rows.)

```sql
-- 1) Description: all English translations that contain the word “toy”.
SELECT DISTINCT
       "product_category_name",
       "product_category_name_english"
FROM   "product_category_name_translation"
WHERE  "product_category_name_english" LIKE '%toy%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Description: Portuguese category names that map to ‘toys’.
SELECT DISTINCT
       "product_category_name"
FROM   "product_category_name_translation"
WHERE  "product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Description: a sample of product_id’s that are classified as toys.
SELECT DISTINCT
       p."product_id"
FROM   "products"                       AS p
JOIN   "product_category_name_translation" AS t
       ON t."product_category_name" = p."product_category_name"
WHERE  t."product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Description: the first 20 order_items that correspond to toy products.
SELECT DISTINCT
       oi."order_id",
       oi."product_id",
       oi."price"
FROM   "order_items"                    AS oi
JOIN   "products"                       AS p
       ON p."product_id" = oi."product_id"
JOIN   "product_category_name_translation" AS t
       ON t."product_category_name" = p."product_category_name"
WHERE  t."product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Description: toy orders together with their purchase timestamp
--                 (restricted to the modelling horizon).
SELECT DISTINCT
       o."order_id",
       date(o."order_purchase_timestamp") AS "sales_date"
FROM   "orders"                          AS o
JOIN   "order_items"                     AS oi  USING ("order_id")
JOIN   "products"                        AS p   ON p."product_id" = oi."product_id"
JOIN   "product_category_name_translation" AS t ON t."product_category_name" = p."product_category_name"
WHERE  t."product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
  AND  date(o."order_purchase_timestamp") BETWEEN '2017-01-01' AND '2018-08-29'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Description: daily toy‑sales counts in the modelling period (first 20 days).
SELECT
       date(o."order_purchase_timestamp")         AS "sales_date",
       COUNT(*)                                   AS "qty"
FROM   "orders"                          AS o
JOIN   "order_items"                     AS oi  USING ("order_id")
JOIN   "products"                        AS p   ON p."product_id" = oi."product_id"
JOIN   "product_category_name_translation" AS t ON t."product_category_name" = p."product_category_name"
WHERE  t."product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
  AND  date(o."order_purchase_timestamp") BETWEEN '2017-01-01' AND '2018-08-29'
GROUP BY "sales_date"
ORDER BY "sales_date"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Description: regression parameters (slope & intercept) that model
--                 Qty ~ a + b * (days since 2017‑01‑01).   Single‑row output.
WITH daily_sales AS (
     SELECT
            date(o."order_purchase_timestamp")              AS sales_date,
            COUNT(*)                                        AS qty,
            julianday(date(o."order_purchase_timestamp")) -
            julianday('2017-01-01')                         AS x
     FROM   "orders" AS o
     JOIN   "order_items"                     AS oi  USING ("order_id")
     JOIN   "products"                        AS p   ON p."product_id" = oi."product_id"
     JOIN   "product_category_name_translation" AS t ON t."product_category_name" = p."product_category_name"
     WHERE  t."product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
       AND  date(o."order_purchase_timestamp") BETWEEN '2017-01-01' AND '2018-08-29'
     GROUP BY sales_date
)
SELECT
       -- counts & sums that will be reused
       COUNT(*)                                            AS n,
       SUM(x)                                              AS sum_x,
       SUM(qty)                                            AS sum_y,
       SUM(x*qty)                                          AS sum_xy,
       SUM(x*x)                                            AS sum_x2,

       -- slope  b  and intercept  a
       (COUNT(*)*SUM(x*qty) - SUM(x)*SUM(qty)) /
       (COUNT(*)*SUM(x*x)  - SUM(x)*SUM(x))                AS slope_b,
       (SUM(qty) - ((COUNT(*)*SUM(x*qty) - SUM(x)*SUM(qty)) /
        (COUNT(*)*SUM(x*x)  - SUM(x)*SUM(x)))*SUM(x)) /
        COUNT(*)                                            AS intercept_a
FROM   daily_sales
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Description: projected daily toy sales between 2018‑12‑01 and 2018‑12‑12.
WITH params AS (      -- copy/paste of query (7) but keeping only a & b
     WITH daily_sales AS (
         SELECT
                date(o."order_purchase_timestamp")              AS sales_date,
                COUNT(*)                                        AS qty,
                julianday(date(o."order_purchase_timestamp")) -
                julianday('2017-01-01')                         AS x
         FROM   "orders" AS o
         JOIN   "order_items"                     AS oi  USING ("order_id")
         JOIN   "products"                        AS p   ON p."product_id" = oi."product_id"
         JOIN   "product_category_name_translation" AS t ON t."product_category_name" = p."product_category_name"
         WHERE  t."product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
           AND  date(o."order_purchase_timestamp") BETWEEN '2017-01-01' AND '2018-08-29'
         GROUP BY sales_date
     )
     SELECT
            (COUNT(*)*SUM(x*qty) - SUM(x)*SUM(qty)) /
            (COUNT(*)*SUM(x*x)  - SUM(x)*SUM(x))                AS b,
            (SUM(qty) - ((COUNT(*)*SUM(x*qty) - SUM(x)*SUM(qty)) /
             (COUNT(*)*SUM(x*x)  - SUM(x)*SUM(x)))*SUM(x)) /
             COUNT(*)                                          AS a
     FROM   daily_sales
),
date_series AS (      -- build the 12‑day horizon we need
     SELECT date('2018-12-01', '+' || (n || ' day')) AS future_date
     FROM   (SELECT 0 AS n UNION ALL SELECT 1 UNION ALL SELECT 2 UNION ALL
             SELECT 3 UNION ALL SELECT 4 UNION ALL SELECT 5 UNION ALL
             SELECT 6 UNION ALL SELECT 7 UNION ALL SELECT 8 UNION ALL
             SELECT 9 UNION ALL SELECT 10 UNION ALL SELECT 11)
)
SELECT
       ds.future_date                                  AS forecast_date,
       p.a + p.b * (julianday(ds.future_date) - julianday('2017-01-01'))
                                                      AS predicted_qty
FROM   date_series  AS ds
CROSS JOIN params   AS p
ORDER BY forecast_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Description: 5‑day symmetric moving average of the predictions
--                 (window: 2 days before + target day + 2 days after).
WITH params AS (      -- same CTE as in (8)
     WITH daily_sales AS (
         SELECT
                date(o."order_purchase_timestamp")              AS sales_date,
                COUNT(*)                                        AS qty,
                julianday(date(o."order_purchase_timestamp")) -
                julianday('2017-01-01')                         AS x
         FROM   "orders" AS o
         JOIN   "order_items"                     AS oi  USING ("order_id")
         JOIN   "products"                        AS p   ON p."product_id" = oi."product_id"
         JOIN   "product_category_name_translation" AS t ON t."product_category_name" = p."product_category_name"
         WHERE  t."product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
           AND  date(o."order_purchase_timestamp") BETWEEN '2017-01-01' AND '2018-08-29'
         GROUP BY sales_date
     )
     SELECT
            (COUNT(*)*SUM(x*qty) - SUM(x)*SUM(qty)) /
            (COUNT(*)*SUM(x*x)  - SUM(x)*SUM(x))                AS b,
            (SUM(qty) - ((COUNT(*)*SUM(x*qty) - SUM(x)*SUM(qty)) /
             (COUNT(*)*SUM(x*x)  - SUM(x)*SUM(x)))*SUM(x)) /
             COUNT(*)                                          AS a
     FROM   daily_sales
),
date_series AS (
     SELECT date('2018-12-01', '+' || (n || ' day')) AS forecast_date
     FROM   (SELECT 0 AS n UNION ALL SELECT 1 UNION ALL SELECT 2 UNION ALL
             SELECT 3 UNION ALL SELECT 4 UNION ALL SELECT 5 UNION ALL
             SELECT 6 UNION ALL SELECT 7 UNION ALL SELECT 8 UNION ALL
             SELECT 9 UNION ALL SELECT 10 UNION ALL SELECT 11)
),
pred AS (
     SELECT
            ds.forecast_date,
            p.a + p.b * (julianday(ds.forecast_date) - julianday('2017-01-01')) AS qty
     FROM   date_series AS ds
     CROSS JOIN params AS p
)
SELECT
       target.forecast_date                                   AS center_date,
       AVG(neigh.qty)                                         AS moving_avg_5d
FROM   pred  AS target
JOIN   pred  AS neigh
       ON neigh.forecast_date BETWEEN date(target.forecast_date,'-2 day')
                                  AND date(target.forecast_date,'+2 day')
WHERE  target.forecast_date BETWEEN '2018-12-03' AND '2018-12-10'
ORDER BY center_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Description: isolate the four requested center‑dates (Dec 5‑8, 2018),
--                  return their 5‑day symmetric moving averages and the
--                  cumulative sum of those four averages.
WITH params AS (      -- identical to CTE in (9)
     WITH daily_sales AS (
         SELECT
                date(o."order_purchase_timestamp")              AS sales_date,
                COUNT(*)                                        AS qty,
                julianday(date(o."order_purchase_timestamp")) -
                julianday('2017-01-01')                         AS x
         FROM   "orders" AS o
         JOIN   "order_items"                     AS oi  USING ("order_id")
         JOIN   "products"                        AS p   ON p."product_id" = oi."product_id"
         JOIN   "product_category_name_translation" AS t ON t."product_category_name" = p."product_category_name"
         WHERE  t."product_category_name_english" = 'toys'
           AND  date(o."order_purchase_timestamp") BETWEEN '2017-01-01' AND '2018-08-29'
         GROUP BY sales_date
     )
     SELECT
            (COUNT(*)*SUM(x*qty) - SUM(x)*SUM(qty)) /
            (COUNT(*)*SUM(x*x)  - SUM(x)*SUM(x))                AS b,
            (SUM(qty) - ((COUNT(*)*SUM(x*qty) - SUM(x)*SUM(qty)) /
             (COUNT(*)*SUM(x*x)  - SUM(x)*SUM(x)))*SUM(x)) /
             COUNT(*)                                          AS a
     FROM   daily_sales
),
date_series AS (      -- we need slightly wider horizon (Dec 03 – Dec 10)
     SELECT date('2018-12-01', '+' || (n || ' day')) AS forecast_date
     FROM   (SELECT 0 AS n UNION ALL SELECT 1 UNION ALL SELECT 2 UNION ALL
             SELECT 3 UNION ALL SELECT 4 UNION ALL SELECT 5 UNION ALL
             SELECT 6 UNION ALL SELECT 7 UNION ALL SELECT 8 UNION ALL
             SELECT 9 UNION ALL SELECT 10 UNION ALL SELECT 11)
),
pred AS (
     SELECT
            ds.forecast_date,
            p.a + p.b * (julianday(ds.forecast_date) - julianday('2017-01-01')) AS qty
     FROM   date_series AS ds
     CROSS JOIN params AS p
),
mov5 AS (      -- compute the 5‑day symmetric moving average
     SELECT
            target.forecast_date                                   AS center_date,
            AVG(neigh.qty)                                         AS moving_avg_5d
     FROM   pred  AS target
     JOIN   pred  AS neigh
            ON neigh.forecast_date BETWEEN date(target.forecast_date,'-2 day')
                                       AND date(target.forecast_date,'+2 day')
     GROUP BY target.forecast_date
)
SELECT
       center_date,
       moving_avg_5d,
       (SELECT SUM(moving_avg_5d)
        FROM   mov5
        WHERE  center_date BETWEEN '2018-12-05' AND '2018-12-08') AS sum_4_avgs
FROM   mov5
WHERE  center_date BETWEEN '2018-12-05' AND '2018-12-08'
ORDER BY center_date
LIMIT 20;
```

Running the last query (10) will return one row per requested date (2018‑12‑05 … 2018‑12‑08) with its 5‑day symmetric moving average and –repeated on every row for convenience– the sum of those four averages, which is the final numeric answer the task asked for.
